  "hints": [
    "If capacity c works, does every capacity larger than c also work?",
    "The answer is between max(weights) and sum(weights). Binary search that range with a yes/no test.",
    "To test capacity c, fill each day greedily until the next parcel would overflow, then start a new day. c works if you use at most days days."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function minCapacity(weights, days) {\n  const fits = cap => {\n    let used = 1\n    let load = 0\n    for (const w of weights) {\n      if (load + w > cap) {\n        used++\n        load = 0\n      }\n      load += w\n    }\n    return used <= days\n  }\n  let lo = 0\n  let hi = 0\n  for (const w of weights) {\n    lo = Math.max(lo, w)\n    hi += w\n  }\n  while (lo < hi) {\n    const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2)\n    if (fits(mid)) hi = mid\n    else lo = mid + 1\n  }\n  return lo\n}\n",
    "py": "def min_capacity(weights, days):\n    def fits(cap):\n        used, load = 1, 0\n        for w in weights:\n            if load + w > cap:\n                used += 1\n                load = 0\n            load += w\n        return used <= days\n\n    lo, hi = max(weights), sum(weights)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if fits(mid):\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo\n",
    "explanation": "Feasibility is monotonic in capacity, so binary search finds the smallest capacity that passes. The check loads each day greedily, which uses the fewest days possible for that capacity. The search range starts at the heaviest parcel because every parcel must fit on some day."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log S)",
    "space": "O(1)",
    "notes": "S is sum(weights). Each feasibility check is a single greedy O(n) pass; greedy filling is optimal because shipping a parcel earlier never leaves more work for later days."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Return the day each parcel ships on for the minimum capacity.",
    "What changes if parcels can be loaded in any order?"
  ]
